model {

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE MACHINE — one command in, background workers out.
  // Every value here was read off CLI/bin/bo, CLI/beads-loop.sh, CLI/loop-log.sh,
  // CLI/forge.sh, hooks/worker-stop.sh, hooks/worker-fail.sh, hooks/hooks.json.
  // The cast only: the story is told by the four walkthroughs in views.c4.
  // Rules for editing these files: ./REFERENCE.md — the code is the only source of truth.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  machine = layer "The machine — one command in, background workers out" {
    summary "One command in, background workers out: the setup skill, the checks, the parameters, the dispatcher, the claim."

    setupSkill = skill "setup-cli — the skill that puts the CLI in your AI agent" {
      summary "One conversation inside your AI agent: it checks the repo, the tickets and the CLI, then installs `bo` on your say-so."
      description """
**What it is:** `skills/setup-cli/SKILL.md` — a conversation, not a command you remember. Say
"setup binary-obelisk" or "install the CLI" to the AI agent and it runs three steps.

| Step | What the agent does |
|---|---|
| 1 · detect | `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, `bd where`, `command -v bo` — the repo, whether tickets live here yet, whether `bo` is on the path |
| 2 · report and branch | reads back the three answers **before** offering anything; no repo → stop and name the folder to `cd` into; no ticket database → hand off to `bead-initialize`; no CLI → step 3; all present → nothing to do, run `bo loop` |
| 3 · install | runs `CLI/install-cli.sh` — only after you confirm: it never blind-runs anything |

**What the installer writes:** one real file at `~/.local/bin/bo` (`CLI/bin/bo`'s launcher), never a
symlink, which finds the plugin folder and execs the real `CLI/bin/bo`. A real file is deliberate: it
outlives an uninstalled plugin and names its own problem — the missing-plugin message prints the `rm`
that clears it and the two ways to get it back. If `~/.local/bin` is not on your path, the installer
prints the exact `export PATH=` line your shell profile is missing.

**Its second write:** `CLI/default.env` copied to `~/.obelisk.env`, the run's parameters at their
defaults — yours to edit by hand. The installer touches that file only when it does not exist; delete
it to get the defaults back. `bo` itself is six commands: `loop`, `plan:epic`, `plan:feature`,
`plan:task`, `overseer`, `commit`.

**Nothing here runs the loop.** The skill's whole job is to make `bo loop` reachable.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/skills/setup-cli/SKILL.md "Source — the setup-cli skill"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/install-cli.sh "Source — CLI/install-cli.sh"
    }

    preflight = noteDoc "The checks — everything that must hold before the first tick" {
      summary "The seven checks that must hold before the first tick; six of them refuse the run outright."
      description """
In the order the code runs them (`CLI/beads-loop.sh` bottom of the file). Six of the seven `exit 1` and no tick
ever happens; a run that cannot report on itself is not a run.

| # | Check | Refuses when |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `requireToolsOnPath` — `bd jq git` plus the first word of `BO_AGENT_COMMAND` | the first one missing is named on stderr |
| 2 | `configureAllowedLabels` — `BO_ALLOWED_LABELS` against the `AGENT` map | **not fatal** — a typo'd list matches no ticket, and the tower sits still while waiting lines keep arriving |
| 3 | `requireNumericSettingsFromEnvironment` | `BO_MAX_PARALLEL` / `BO_POLL_S` / `BO_MAX_TICKETS` set to anything but digits — never defaulted silently, because they govern unattended workers |
| 4 | `requireGateFlagsAreSwitches` — `BO_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` and `BO_AGENT_EPIC_GATES`, each read by `configureFromEnvironment` | either one is anything but `0` or `1` — the line names the flag and says what each value means: `0` a human closes that gate, `1` the lead agent does. A half-open gate tier is not a state the loop will guess its way through |
| 5 | `requireUsablePollInterval` — `BO_POLL_S` must divide 60 | the permitted list is printed: `1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60`; the waiting lines are rate-limited at one per minute, so a poll that does not divide the minute makes that cadence a lie |
| 6 | `requireBeadsProject` — `bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an **array** | `bd` walks **up** from the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`, so a database in the environment does not save a wrong directory |
| 7 | `createRunLogDirectory` — `mkdir -p` the run's dir, then seed `claims errors waiting results closes failures` | fatal on purpose: a run that cannot write its logs cannot raise an alarm |

`bo` itself runs check 6 and one before it — `requireEngine`, that `../beads-loop.sh` is actually there — so a
broken install names itself instead of failing deep in a tick.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L53-L155 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 53–155"
    }

    knobs = knobPanel "The run's parameters (env, read once at startup)" {
      summary "The run's parameters, read once at startup out of `~/.obelisk.env`; what you export yourself still wins."
      description """
Both engines source `CLI/user-env.sh` and call `loadObeliskEnv` as their first act. It takes only
`BO_*=` lines out of `~/.obelisk.env` (`CLI/default.env` copied there on install), expands `$HOME` in a
value, and **exports only what is unset** — so a value you exported yourself still beats the file.
`BO_ENV_FILE` points it elsewhere, which is the whole test hook; no file at all is a normal state, and
the engines' own `:-` defaults are the floor under it. `CLI/default.env` is documentation that happens
to be installed, so it and the engines are kept in step by hand.

| Knob | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `BO_AGENT_COMMAND` | `claude` | the command that runs an agent — the worker spawn, the worker count, and the three planners all go through it; split on spaces, so it may carry its own arguments |
| `BO_AGENT_PLUGIN` | `binary-obelisk` | the plugin the agents ship in: every spawn names one as `<plugin>:<agent>`, and a bare name does not resolve to a plugin agent |
| `BO_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` | `0` | does the **lead agent** close a feature gate for you. `0` = a human closes it; `1` = the loop dispatches the lead agent at a ready feature gate. Anything else and the run refuses to start |
| `BO_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` | `0` | the same switch for the epic gate alone — one flag per tier, because a feature gate and the epic gate are different amounts of trust |
| `BO_MAX_PARALLEL` | `4` | workers spawned at once; the cap is recounted from `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` every tick |
| `BO_POLL_S` | `3` | seconds between ticks; **must divide 60** or the run refuses to start, because the idle rate limit is `60 / BO_POLL_S` ticks |
| `BO_MAX_TICKETS` | `0` | stop once N tickets have closed this run — counted off the run's `closes` file, so the loop keeps ticking while it waits; 0 = endless |
| `BO_LOG_ROOT` | `~/.binary-obelisk/logs` | parent of the one timestamped dir this run writes |
| `BO_ALLOWED_LABELS` | unset or `*` = the whole AGENT set | a comma list narrows which labels may dispatch |
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L39-L59 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 39–59"
    }

    dispatcher = dispatcher "beads-loop.sh — the dispatcher" {
      summary "The engine: a tick dispatches any ready gate its tier flag opens, then claims tickets, derives their branches off the id, spawns workers."
      description """
**What it is:** the engine `bo loop` runs. A `while true` loop: test the ticket cap, `tick`, `sleep $BO_POLL_S`,
again. It ends by itself only when `BO_MAX_TICKETS` tickets have closed this run — the test is against the run's
`closes` file, not against the tick count, so a run waiting on workers still ticking is never cut off.
It is the only thing in the system that claims, dispatches, publishes the epic branch, or owns folders.

One tick, in the order the code runs it (`tick()`):

| # | Step | Reads | On failure |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `branchesCurrentlyAtWork` — the branches already at work, read **before** claiming so the ticket in hand is never counted against itself | `bd list --status in_progress --limit 0 --json` → `.[] \| .id`, each one through `resolveTicketBranches`, `sort -u` | — |
| 1 | **dispatch any ready gate its tier flag opens** — `dispatchReadyGates`, before a single ticket is claimed. Returns at once when neither `BO_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` nor `BO_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` is `1` | `bd list --include-gates --ready --type gate --limit 0 --json` → `.id`; the tier is `gateTierOf`, never a key | see the gate rows below |
| 2 | capacity check | `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json`, background sessions whose `cwd` starts with `<tower>/.claude/worktrees` or equals the tower | waiting `max_parallel` / `max_parallel_stalled` |
| 3 | **claim** | `bd ready --claim --json` — atomic: assignee + `in_progress` + a 5-minute lease | `bd-failure`; an empty answer is a waiting line, not an error |
| 4 | match labels against the `AGENT` map — **exactly one** may match | `.[0].labels` | `label-ambiguous`, left claimed |
| 5 | derive the branch and the PR target off the **id** | `resolveTicketBranches <id>` — `${id%.*}` is the feature gate whose `branch` the work lands on, `${id%%.*}` the epic gate whose `branch` is `prTarget`; `prTarget` is `main` when the owner is the epic gate itself. Nothing is walked and no key on the ticket is read | `no-epic-gate ticket=…` (`no-dot` · `not-a-gate` · `step=too-deep`), `gate-unworkable ticket=…` (`owner-carries-no-branch` · `epic-carries-no-branch`), left claimed |
| 6 | one pair of hands per branch | is `branch` in the busy set? | `bd unclaim`, try the next claim, at most `BO_MAX_PARALLEL + 2` attempts a tick |
| 7 | publish the epic branch + PR if the gate has none | `ensureEpicGatePr <gate-id>` — the branch is born with a commit of its own, so it is never `main`'s own commit | `epic-pr-failed step=…`, `bd unclaim` |
| 8 | make the branch's folder | `ensureWorktree branch prTarget` | `worktree-failed`, left claimed |
| 9 | spawn | `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND --bg --agent <plugin>:<agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions`, cwd **inside** the worktree | `worker-not-dispatched`, `bd unclaim` |
| 10 | one log line, move on | `logTo claims \"reason=dispatched\"` | — |

**The gate half of the tick** (`dispatchOneGate`, step 1) is the only place a worker is spawned without a
label. A gate never appears in `bd ready`, so it is claimed **by status**: `bd update <gate> --status
in_progress --if-status open`, and the `--if-status` is both the race guard and what takes the gate off the
ready list until its lead agent dies.

| Step | Reads | On failure |
|---|---|---|
| the tier is worked out, then switched on | `gateTierOf` — `bd dep list <gate> --direction up -t blocks --json`: nothing depends on it → `epic`, anything → `feature`; matched against the two flags | a tier whose flag is `0` is skipped in silence |
| derive the gate's branch and target | `resolveTicketBranches <gate>` — the gate's own `branch`, and the epic gate's `branch` (or `main`) as the target | waiting `gate_unworkable gate=… step=…` |
| one pair of hands per branch | is the gate's branch in the busy set? | waiting `branch_busy held=1 gate=…` |
| **claim it** | `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open` | `gate-claim-lost`, no lead agent spawned |
| make the branch's folder | `ensureWorktree gateBranch gatePrTarget` | `worktree-failed step=gate`, and the gate is put back `open` |
| spawn the **lead agent** | `--agent <plugin>:lead`, label on the line is `gate:<tier>` | `worker-not-dispatched gate=…`, the gate put back `open` |
| one log line | `logTo claims \"reason=dispatched kind=gate gate=… tier=…\"` | — |

**What it never does:** it never polls a worker, never reads worker output, never merges, never ends
itself, never closes a ticket, and never derives an agent name from a label. It also runs **no
`bd reclaim`**: a lease that lapses leaves its ticket parked `in_progress`, off the frontier — sweeping
claims back is the supervisor's job, not the loop's.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh "Source — CLI/beads-loop.sh"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/.claude/design.yaml "Source — the design model"
    }

    agentMap = noteDoc "The AGENT map — the only label→worker binding" {
      summary "The seven labels that dispatch, each bound to one worker; nothing else picks a worker."
      description """
```bash
declare -A AGENT=(
    [backend-logic]=engineer      [frontend-ui]=frontend
    [unit-tests]=tester-unit      [feature-test]=tester-feature
    [epic-e2e-test]=tester-e2e    [feature-review]=review
    [epic-review]=review
)

leadAgentName="lead"   # the one worker no key of this map reaches
```

**Three names, never interchangeable** — collapsing any two is how a tower silently dispatches nothing:

| Name | What it is | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **category** | a display hint in `bd list`; names a plan-task template | `backend-logic`, `frontend-ux`, `unit-tests`, `feature-test`, `epic-e2e-test`, `review` |
| **label** | written with `bd create -l`; a ticket may carry any number, but **exactly one** must be a key of this map | the seven keys above, bar none |
| **agent** | a real file in `agents/` | `engineer`, `frontend`, `tester-unit`, `tester-feature`, `tester-e2e`, `review`, `lead` |

The keys **are** the closed label set: `isLabel` requires membership here *and* in the allowed set.
Zero or two matches on a claim is the tower's bug, not the loop's — it never guesses a specialist.
`review` answers two labels; the three test labels are three **tiers**, and the label — never `--agent` —
is what carries the tier, so a mis-labelled ticket gets the wrong depth of coverage and no worker can
tell you it did. Note `frontend-ui` is the **label** while `frontend-ux` is only its category: a ticket
labelled with its category matches nothing. There is no merge label at either tier — nothing assembles a
feature or an epic, each branch is merged by the **lead agent** standing on the gate above it, and the
lead agent is reached by the gate dispatch, not by a key of this map.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L20-L30 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 20–30"
    }

    spawnEnv = knobPanel "BO_* — the environment the spawn carries" {
      summary "The eight BO_ variables the spawn carries, which tell both hooks what to finish."
      description """
Set on the `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND --bg` line and nothing else: this is how the hooks know which ticket to finish
and what to say about it. Without them the worker just stops and its ticket sits `in_progress`.

| Var | Value | Who reads it |
|---|---|---|
| `BO_TICKET` | `the back-end ticket` | both hooks — and it is the **only** test that tells a dispatched worker from any other session in any other project |
| `BO_BRANCH` | `feature/add-record` | Stop hook: commit here, push here, refuse if `git branch --show-current` disagrees |
| `BO_PR_TARGET` | `epic/record-box`, or `main` at the epic tier | Stop hook: the target of this branch's PR — derived off the ticket's id by the loop, carried through the claims line as `target=` |
| `BO_AGENT` | `engineer`, or `lead` on a gate dispatch | both hooks, so an alarm names who was doing the work |
| `BO_LABEL` | `backend-logic`, or `gate:<tier>` on a gate dispatch | both hooks, so an alarm names what chose that worker |
| `BO_PROJECT_DIR` | the tower root, where `.beads` lives | both hooks — **the worktree is not the tower** |
| `BO_LOG_DIR` | this run's log dir | both hooks: alarms land in `$BO_LOG_DIR/errors` |
| `BO_DONE_FILE` | `$BO_LOG_DIR/done-<ticket with / to +>` | the worker touches it; the Stop hook is fail-closed on it |

`BO_LOG_QUIET=1` is set by the hooks before they source the line format: a hook must never print
into a session. The dispatcher leaves it unset, so its own lines also go to the terminal.

**The tenth argument, `kind`, is not an environment variable.** `spawnWorker` takes it as `${10:-task}` and
writes it into the dispatch context file beside the other eight — `ticket branch pr_target agent label tower
done_file kind` — and the Stop hook reads it back as `BO_WORK_KIND="${claimKind:-${kind:-task}}"`, off the
claims line first and the context file only as a fallback. It is the only thing that tells the hook it is
finishing a **gate** rather than a task.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh#L525-L545 "Source — beads-loop.sh lines 525–545"
    }

    claim = cliTool "bd ready --claim — the hand that takes one ticket" {
      summary "bd ready --claim — takes one unblocked ticket atomically and starts its five-minute lease."
      description """
**What it is:** the one atomic read in the system, and the only reason the loop can run two places at once
without stealing its own work. `bd ready --claim --json` picks the single first unblocked ticket and, in the
same statement, stamps an assignee, moves it to `in_progress`, and starts a **five-minute lease**.

| Property | Consequence |
|---|---|
| atomic | a bare `bd ready` is a peek, not a take — two loops reading with it would both work the same ticket |
| one ticket per call | the tick loops: claim, work it or put it back, claim again — at most `BO_MAX_PARALLEL + 2` times a tick |
| an **empty** answer is not an error | that is a waiting line: `frontier_empty` if nothing is waiting elsewhere, `tower_stalled waiting=N` if work exists behind a blocker |
| the lease is the only watchdog | a lapsed lease parks the ticket `in_progress` and off the frontier — nothing in this plugin runs `bd reclaim`, so sweeping claims back is a human's act |
| `bd unclaim` puts a claim back | the loop does it exactly twice: the branch is already at work, or the spawn died |

A claim is a JSON object, and **two** fields decide everything downstream: `.id` (which two gates it sits
under, and therefore which branch the work lands on and which its pull request aims at — `resolveTicketBranches`
reads both off the id, and a second query fetches the gates' own `branch` keys) and `.labels` (which worker, if
exactly one matches the map). Nothing on the claim's `.metadata` is read at all. Anything else on the ticket —
title, description, notes — the loop never reads.

**A gate never appears here.** `bd ready` does not list one, so a gate is never taken by this command; the
loop moves it to `in_progress` itself with `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open`, which is
step 1 of the tick and the only claim in the system made by status.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/beads-loop.sh "Source — CLI/beads-loop.sh"
    }

  }  // machine

  logRun = logStore "~/.binary-obelisk/logs/<date>_<time> — one dir per run" {
    summary "One timestamped directory per run, holding every file that run writes."
    description """
**What it is:** the central home for everything a run leaves behind. The engine seeds **six** files up
front (`claims errors waiting results closes failures`), so a missing file and an empty file look the
same and neither hides a silent failure — `closes` and `failures` are written by the hooks, possibly
long after the run ended. Three more appear as needed (`bd-stderr`, `claude-stderr`) and one pair per
worker (`spawn-<ticket>`, `done-<ticket>`).

**One line format, sourced by the engine and by both hooks** (`CLI/loop-log.sh`), or the files cannot be
read against each other:

```
<TAG padded to 8> <ISO time>\trun=… repo=… ticket=… label=… agent=… branch=… target=… session=… worktree=… <detail>
```

The nine keys are on **every line of every file**, `\"-\"` for whatever is not known at that moment — an
absent field would be indistinguishable from a field nobody set, and an error line is worth nothing if
it cannot say what it happened to. That shared key set is what makes the files **JOIN**: an `errors` line
names the ticket, label, agent, branch, target, repo and session, and the same keys lead from it into
`claims`, `closes` or `failures` by `grep` alone. Anything outside the nine — a `step=`, a `gate=`, an
`exit=`, a `msg=` — is passed as **detail**; the key set is closed, or the joining stops working.

```
CLAIMS   2026-11-04T09:12:03\trun=2026-11-04_09-12-01 repo=/home/dev/record-box ticket=back-end label=backend-logic agent=engineer branch=feature/add-record target=epic/record-box session=287d3c4c worktree=/home/dev/record-box/.claude/worktrees/feature+add-record reason=dispatched
```
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/loop-log.sh "Source — CLI/loop-log.sh"

    fClaims = logFile "claims" {
      summary "Every ticket taken, and every one put back."
      description """
Every ticket taken, and every one put back. `reason=dispatched` on a spawn, `reason=branch-busy unclaimed`
on a put-back, and `gate=… reason=epic-published gateBranch=… pr=…` when the loop publishes the epic branch —
that one line names no ticket of its own, because the identity the tick already carries is on it.

A **gate** dispatch is the dispatched line with three keys added: `reason=dispatched kind=gate gate=<id>
tier=<epic|feature>`, and its fixed identity already reads `ticket=<gate id> agent=lead label=gate:<tier>
target=<the gate's `prTarget`>`. Those three keys are the only place a reader can tell a gate dispatch from a task
dispatch — the nine identity keys look identical either way, and the Stop hook reads `kind=` off this very
line to decide which path to take.
"""
    }
    fErrors = logFile "errors" {
      summary "Every failure, whoever saw it, and never rate-limited."
      description """
The alarm: every failure, whoever saw it. **Never rate-limited** — a waiting line is capped at one a minute
per reason, an error never is.
"""
    }
    fWaiting = logFile "waiting" {
      summary "The quiet ticks, one line a minute per reason."
      description """
The quiet ticks, **one a minute per reason** (`60 / BO_POLL_S` ticks), each carrying
`working=<busy sessions>` and `next=<the id a bare bd ready would name>`.
"""
    }
    fCloses = logFile "closes" {
      summary "One line per ticket that landed, with its pull request url."
      description """
The Stop hook's proof of work: one line per ticket that landed, `url=<the PR>`. A **gate** close carries one
extra key, `url=<the PR> gate=true` — the same url, because the lead agent merged that branch's own pull
request rather than opening a new one, and the hook found it whatever state it was in.
"""
    }
    fFailures = logFile "failures" {
      summary "A session that died mid-turn: from which state, which kind, its last message."
      description """
The StopFailure hook's account of a session that died mid-turn: `from=<state> kind=<.error> msg=\"<last assistant message>\"`.
"""
    }
    fResults = logFile "results" {
      summary "The run's tally of ticks, claims and tickets closed, at the ticket cap or on Ctrl-C."
      description """
The run's tally — `ticks=N claimed=M done=K` — at `BO_MAX_TICKETS`, or on Ctrl-C (`onInterrupt`). The cap line
carries a `note=` saying the run was configured to end there; the Ctrl-C line does not, so the two are
told apart without guessing. `done=K` is the count off this run's `closes` file.
"""
    }
    fBdStderr = logFile "bd-stderr" {
      summary "Stderr of the last claim, quoted on a claim failure."
      description """
Stderr of the last `bd ready --claim`, captured on the claim line itself; its last line is quoted as
`msg=` on a `bd-failure` alarm.
"""
    }
    fClaudeStderr = logFile "claude-stderr" {
      summary "Stderr of the last capacity count."
      description """
Stderr of the last `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` capacity count.
"""
    }
    fSpawn = logFile "spawn-<ticket>" {
      summary "That one worker's raw spawn output, which holds its session id."
      description """
That one worker's raw spawn output, which holds its session id (`backgrounded · 287d3c4c`), read back by
`sessionFromSpawn`. It used to be one shared `last-spawn` that every spawn overwrote, so the id survived
only for the run's final worker — the one thing a human needs to attach to, read, or stop.
"""
    }
    fDone = logFile "done-<ticket>" {
      summary "The worker's own marker saying its brief is complete; the Stop hook is fail-closed on it."
      description """
The worker's own declaration that its brief is complete, created by `touch \"$BO_DONE_FILE\"` as its very
last action. The engine `rm -f`s it before spawning, so a marker can only mean *this* session; the Stop
hook refuses to land anything without it.
"""
    }
  }

  stopHook = hook "worker-stop.sh — the Stop hook, fail-closed" {
    summary "The closer: commit, push, the branch's pull request, close — and it refuses sixteen ways first."
    description """
**What it is:** the closer. Registered in `hooks/hooks.json` on the `Stop` event with `timeout: 300`, so it
fires in **every session of every project** the plugin is enabled in — `BO_TICKET` is what tells a dispatched
worker apart from everything else, and a session the loop did not spawn leaves on the first line.

```
worker says it is done → `bo commit` onto its feature's branch → push → the branch's PR →
write the PR url onto the ticket's pr metadata → close it
```

It refuses **sixteen** ways, each one `exit 0` with the ticket untouched, in the order the code checks them —
they are walked one by one in the second walkthrough. The gate of the whole design is `not-declared-done`:
no marker, no commit, no push, no PR, no close.

**The commit is the worker's own command, not a private one:** `stageTheWork` first asks whether anything is
left to land — usually the worker committed it all as it went, and then nothing is — and `commitTheTask` runs
`bo commit` in the worktree with the ticket's title and a `Ticket:` line as the message, so a task's last
commit is the same shape as all the others. `git diff --cached --quiet` proving the index came back clean is
what `reason=commit-failed` means. The hook then pushes for itself: cheap when the branch is current, and it
is the hook's own guarantee that the branch is on the forge before a PR is opened off it — which is why
`reason=push-failed` is a separate alarm from `reason=commit-failed`. `bo` is in `requireTools` for this
reason; `reason=missing-tool tool=bo` is the alarm when the CLI is not installed.

**One branch, one pull request, forever:** the branch's first task creates the PR, and every later task finds
it by `gh pr list --head <branch> --base <target> --state open` and reuses it — which is why the branch, not
the ticket, is the identity of the work.

**Two paths, decided by one key.** Everything above the `BO_WORK_KIND` test is shared: guard, marker, branch,
commit, push, forge. A claim whose `kind=gate` (read off the dispatched claims line, falling back to the
context file, defaulting to `task`) then takes a different road: it looks the branch's PR up **at any state**
— `gh pr list … --state all` / `glab mr list … --all`, not `--state open` — because the lead agent has just
merged it and an open-only lookup would find nothing. With no PR at all the hook raises
`reason=pr-not-found` and leaves the gate claimed: there was nothing there to merge. With one, it annotates
the gate with the url, closes the **gate** as its claimant, writes `closes url=… gate=true`, removes the
context file and exits. **It never opens a new PR on the gate path** — the lead agent merged the branch
through the forge, and a second PR off a merged branch is a lie about work nobody did.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/hooks/worker-stop.sh "Source — hooks/worker-stop.sh"
  }

  failHook = hook "worker-fail.sh — the StopFailure hook" {
    summary "What runs when a worker's session dies mid-turn: the reason onto the ticket, then park it."
    description """
**What it is:** what happens when a worker's session dies on an API error mid-turn (`StopFailure`, `timeout: 60`).
A dead worker stops heartbeating and its lease lapses — and nothing in this plugin runs `bd reclaim`, so an
unwatched lapse simply leaves the ticket parked `in_progress`, invisible to `bd ready`, until a person moves
it. This hook takes the ticket somewhere a human will see it, with the reason attached, on purpose.

```bash
bd update \"$ticket\" --append-notes \"Worker session <id> failed: <kind>
Last message: <last assistant message>\"
bd update \"$ticket\" --status failed-agent
logTo failures \"from=<state> kind=<kind> msg=\"…\"\"
```

The reason is **never polled, parsed out of a log, or asked of the model**: the harness fires this hook itself
and hands the text on stdin — `.error` is its coarse label for the kind of death (often just `unknown`),
`.last_assistant_message` is the last thing the session produced, which is where the real text is (for local
inference, typically an `API Error:` line from the proxy). The reason is written **before** the status changes:
a ticket parked in failure with nothing said about why is worse than one left `in_progress`. Every step is
*attempted, never forced*: one that fails leaves the ticket exactly where it was and logs why.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/hooks/worker-fail.sh "Source — hooks/worker-fail.sh"
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/hooks/hooks.json "Source — hooks/hooks.json (Stop 300 s, StopFailure 60 s)"
  }

  beads = beadsStore "beads DB (bd) — the tower" {
    summary "The database holding every gate, ticket and edge — the loop's only runtime input."
    description """
**What it is:** the loop's **only runtime input**. None of the design docs or CLAUDE.md files are read at
runtime; the graph — every gate, ticket, `blocks` edge, and the metadata keys — lives here. The commands
that read and write it are in the toolbelt; what lives here is the **state**, and the state is two keys.

**The two metadata keys, and only two.** One is the plan's, one is the loop's or the hook's:

| Fact | Where it lives | Who reads it |
|---|---|---|
| `branch` | on a **gate only** — the epic gate and each feature gate carry their own, written once by the `plan-*` agent that owns the tier. No task, suite or review carries one | the loop, through `readFieldOf <gate> branch`, on the gate the ticket's id names — then handed down as `BO_BRANCH`; nothing parses text for it |
| `pr` | the url, written by the loop (onto the epic gate) or the Stop hook (onto any ticket whose work landed) — never by a plan agent | a human, and the hook when it looks for the PR already on a branch |

**Everything else about a ticket's git world is derived, never stored.** For `bd-cub.2.1`: `${id%.*}` is the
feature gate whose `branch` the work lands on, and `${id%%.*}` the epic gate whose `branch` is the
**`prTarget`** — `main` when the owner is the epic gate itself. Which tier a gate is comes from the graph:
`bd dep list <gate> --direction up -t blocks` empty → epic, anything → feature. A stored copy of either is a
fact that can disagree with the gate that decides it, which is why neither is a key.

**There is no `parent` key, no `parent-child` edge, and no `--parent` at create time.** Parenthood is what
made a gate unenforceable: beads refuses a `blocks` edge from an ancestor to its own descendant. With nothing
to walk, the id is the only thing pointing at a gate — so every ticket below the epic is created with an
explicit dotted `--id`, and a flat one reaches no gate: the loop refuses it, `ERROR reason=no-epic-gate
ticket=<id>`, and leaves it claimed on purpose. A gate is a **custom type** and must be registered
(`bd config set types.custom \"gate\"`), as is the parking status
(`bd config set status.custom \"failed-agent:frozen\"`): `bd` does not reject an unregistered type or status, it
**accepts it silently and then refuses it**, so a broken gate and a working one look identical. Check
`bd types` before you trust one.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/skills/bead-initialize/SKILL.md "Source — the bead-initialize skill"
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/.claude/design.yaml "Source — the design model"
  }

  forge = forge "origin — GitHub or GitLab" {
    summary "The remote the tower publishes to: one pull request per branch."
    description """
**What it is:** the forge the tower publishes to. `main` at the bottom, one PR per branch above it:
`feature/<slug>` → PR → the epic branch, `epic/<slug>` → PR → `main`.

**Which forge it is, is settled three ways, in this order** (`detectForge` in `CLI/forge.sh`, used by both the
loop and the Stop hook): an honest host name in the remote URL (`*gitlab*`, `*github*`); then the refs that host
actually publishes, because only GitHub serves `refs/pull/` and only GitLab serves
`refs/merge-requests|pipelines|environments/`; then a machine holding **exactly one** of `gh`/`glab`.
Where all three are ambiguous the hook refuses to guess and logs `forge-unknown` — a wrong guess pushes
a branch to the wrong forge.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/CLI/forge.sh "Source — CLI/forge.sh"
  }

  toolbelt = layer "The commands the loop shells out to" {
    summary "The commands the loop shells out to: the ticket tool, the agent command, git, the forge tool, the folder."

    bdCmds = noteDoc "bd — the only reads and writes the tower answers to" {
      summary "The ticket commands the tower answers to, and why each one is that one."
      description """
| Command | Why this one |
|---|---|
| `bd ready --claim --json` | the **only** sanctioned claim: atomic assignee + `in_progress` + 5-minute lease |
| `bd ready --json` (peek) | used once, inside `waiting()`, purely to name `next=` |
| `bd unclaim <id>` | put a claim back — branch already at work, or the spawn died |
| `bd heartbeat <id>` | the worker keeps its lease alive; it never closes its own ticket |
| `bd list --status in_progress --limit 0 --json` | the `.id`s of everything in progress, each through `resolveTicketBranches` → the branches currently at work, read before the claim so the ticket in hand is never counted against itself |
| `bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0 --json` | the probe that tells `frontier_empty` from `tower_stalled` |
| `bd show --json <id>` | read one ticket back — its status, its title (`--long`, the commit message), its `issue_type` (is the id's truncation a gate?), and a gate's `branch` |
| `bd update <id> --set-metadata k=v` | **merges** — which is why writing `pr` never disturbs the plan's `branch` |
| `bd update <id> --status <s> --if-status <from>` | **the gate claim**, and the only conditional write in the system: `moveGateTo <gate> in_progress open` is the take, `moveGateTo <gate> open in_progress` is the put-back, and a non-zero exit (the gate was no longer in `<from>`) is the `gate-claim-lost` alarm. A gate never reaches `bd ready`, so this is the only way it moves |
| `bd close <id> --session <id> --reason …` | only the Stop hook, only on the done marker — a task or a gate, never by the worker itself |
| `bd list --include-gates --ready --type gate --limit 0 --json` | the ready gates, `.id` alone — the only listing that shows a gate at all, and the input to `dispatchReadyGates`. The tier is not on the row; `gateTierOf` works it out |
| `bd dep list <gate> --direction up -t blocks --json` | the gate's **dependents** — empty means nothing waits on it, so it is the epic gate; anything means a feature gate. The only way the system knows a tier |
| `bd create -t gate --id <epic>.<n> --metadata '{"branch":…}'` / `bd create -t task -l <label> --id <gate>.<m>` | the plan agents' build: a gate carries **zero** labels and the `branch` key alone, a task exactly one dispatching label and **no metadata at all**. Every ticket below the epic carries an explicit dotted `--id` — a flat one reaches no gate. There is **no `--parent`** anywhere in a tower |
| `bd dep add <blocked> <blocker> --type blocks` | the **one edge type in a tower**, read "blocker blocks blocked"; what a tier *is* = the tickets that block its gate |
| `bd where` | which DB a directory resolves — `bd` walks **up** and ignores `BEADS_DIR` |

A gate is a **custom type** and must be registered (`bd config set types.custom \"gate\"`): `bd` does not reject
an unregistered type, it **accepts and hides it**, so a broken gate and a working one look identical. Check
`bd types` before you trust one.
"""
    }

    claudeCli = cliTool "$BO_AGENT_COMMAND (claude) — the command every agent runs through" {
      summary "The one command every agent runs through: the worker spawn, the worker count, and the three planners."
      description """
`BO_AGENT_COMMAND`, default `claude` — the one command every agent in the system runs through, so
pointing it at your own build moves all of them. Three uses:

- `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND --bg --agent <agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions \"<the six rules>\"` —
  the worker spawn, run with `cd` **inside** the branch's worktree, and deliberately **no `-w`** —
  `claude --worktree` only ever *creates* one, and this branch already owns its own.
- `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` — the capacity count, recounted fresh every tick: background
  sessions whose `cwd` starts with `<tower>/.claude/worktrees` or equals the tower.
- `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND --agent <planner> \"<prompt>\"` — the three plan agents, foreground, through
  `CLI/plan-agent.sh`, which is what `bo plan:epic`, `bo plan:feature` and `bo plan:task` run.

It is split on spaces, so it may carry its own arguments, and its first word is what the startup check
looks for on the PATH.

Workers run `bypassPermissions` unattended, so this loop only ever belongs in a repo you fully trust.
"""
    }

    gitCli = cliTool "git — worktrees, fetch, push" {
      summary "The git the loop runs: is a branch checked out, make its folder, fetch, push."
      description """
The loop owns the folders, because a worktree belongs to a **branch**, not to a ticket.

- `branchAlreadyCheckedOut`: parse `git worktree list --porcelain` for `refs/heads/<branch>` — a branch
  already checked out somewhere is reused, never re-added.
- `ensureWorktree <branch> <prTarget>` → `git worktree add -B <branch> <path> <start>`, under
  `<tower>/.claude/worktrees/`, where the folder name is the branch name with `/` → `+`
  (`feature/add-record` → `feature+add-record`).
- `<start>` is the **first** of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` that resolves —
  a feature branch starts where it is going to land.
- `git -C <tower> fetch --quiet origin` before each lookup, and the loop's one push —
  `git -C <tower> push --quiet origin <start>:refs/heads/<gateBranch>`, publishing an epic branch that has none.
- **Nothing else here commits.** `git add -A`, `git commit -m \"agent:branch:message\"` and
  `git push -u origin <branch>` all live inside `bo commit` (`CLI/commit.sh`) — the only way anything
  commits in this plugin. The agent is `BO_AGENT`, `manual` when a person typed it; the branch is
  `git symbolic-ref --short HEAD`, and a detached HEAD is refused because a branch it cannot name is a
  subject it cannot write.
"""
    }

    forgeCli = cliTool "gh / glab" {
      summary "gh or glab, whichever the remote settled on: find the branch's pull request, open one if none."
      description """
Whichever `detectForge` settled on. Both are used the same way: list the PR already on this branch pair,
and create one only when there is none. Only a URL matching `/(pull|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` counts as a
pull request — an error message quoting a docs URL is not one.
"""
    }

    worktree = branchBox "the branch's folder — .claude/worktrees/<branch with / → +>" {
      summary "One folder per branch, where every ticket of that feature works in turn."
      description """
One folder per **branch**, not per ticket, which is why the loop owns it: `feature/add-record` lives at
`.claude/worktrees/feature+add-record`, and every ticket of that feature works in the same folder, one after
the other, each one's commit landing on the last.

```
git worktree list --porcelain          is this branch checked out somewhere already? reuse it
git worktree add -B <branch> <path> <start>
<start> = origin/<branch> | origin/<prTarget> | <prTarget> | HEAD   — the first that resolves
git -C <tower> fetch --quiet origin    before each lookup
```

A feature branch starts where it is going to land. The worker is spawned with its cwd **inside** this folder,
and it lands its own finished pieces here as it goes — `bo commit`, one commit per piece (the rule is on the
workers layer) — never a bare `git commit` or `git push`, never a branch of its own, so two hands never write
one branch. The tower root (where `.beads` lives) is `BO_PROJECT_DIR`: **the worktree is not the tower**.
"""
    }

  }  // toolbelt

  human = human "Human (Brodie)" {
    summary "Closes the gates and nothing else — until a tier's flag hands that gate to the lead agent."
    description """
**What it is:** the one who closes a **gate**, unless that tier's switch says otherwise. A *task* is closed by
the Stop hook, once its worker touches the done marker — the human never reaches for a task, and a worker that
closes its own ticket is a bug, because the hook walks past an already-closed ticket and lands nothing. The
human reads the worker's report, verifies the branch and the PR, and closes the gate above it. There is **no
`human` agent** — the person enters through gates, and nothing is ever spawned for them. A claimable ticket
assigned to a human is a model bug.

Both gates are the human's by default: `BO_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES=0` and `BO_AGENT_EPIC_GATES=0`, and a gate stays
open until someone verifies it. Setting a tier's flag to `1` hands *that* tier to the **lead agent**, which the
loop spawns for a ready gate and whose Stop hook closes it — the other tier is still the human's, which is why
there are two switches and not one. The two are not the same amount of trust: a feature gate merges one flow
into the epic branch, the epic gate merges the whole thing into `main`.

`bd ready` never shows a gate, so a tower **stalls** at an open one until it is verified — by a person, or by
the lead agent proving the tier out loud. That is the design, not a bug — it is the only place a claim loop
cannot fake understanding.
"""
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE WORKERS — the spokes. Each is one agent file, dispatched by one label.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  workers = layer "The workers" {
    summary "The seven workers: six are reached by a label, the seventh — the lead — only by a gate."
    description """
**Every worker reads its ticket and writes on it.** All seven load `bead-read` to take in the brief and
`bead-comment` to write what remains onto the ticket — which is a third surface, beside the final message and
the pull request: the ticket is where a worker leaves what the next reader cannot infer from the diff.

**The contract is in the agent file.** Every tower agent carries a `## The contract` section — keep the
five-minute lease alive with `bd heartbeat <ticket>` between long steps, never close your own ticket (the Stop
hook closes it once you declare done, and a ticket already closed makes the hook walk past it, landing nothing),
write what remains as a comment, spawn nothing, and `touch $BO_DONE_FILE` as the very last action and only
when the brief is genuinely complete. The `lead` agent states the same rules inside its own steps and hard
rules rather than in a section of that name — `bd heartbeat <gate>`, never `bd close` not even its own, spawn
nothing, `touch $BO_DONE_FILE` last. No skill holds these rules; the loop's spawn prompt repeats them, which is the
safety net for a worker run outside the loop.

**The five that build, commit as they go.** `engineer`, `frontend`, `tester-unit`, `tester-feature` and
`tester-e2e` each carry a `## Commit As You Go` section running `BO_AGENT=<agent> bo commit <what you just
did>` after every major change that stands on its own — one commit per piece, so the pull request shows the
work as it happens instead of one blob at the end. The name is passed rather than inherited, so a worker run
outside the loop still commits named for itself. `lead` and `review` have no such section: `review` commits
nothing at all, and the lead commits only what it had to resolve where the target moved under the branch.
"""
    engineer = worker "engineer" {
      summary "Business logic, endpoints, hooks, types, error factories; never tests, JSX or CSS."
      description """
**Label `backend-logic` → `engineer`.** Business logic, endpoints, hooks, types, error factories.
Never writes tests, JSX or CSS. Skills: `bead-read` and the four standards; the worker contract lives in the agent file itself.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/engineer.md "Source — agents/engineer.md"
    }
    testerUnit = worker "tester-unit" {
      summary "Micro tier: one back-end function alone — every branch, boundary and error it throws."
      description """
**Label `unit-tests` → `tester-unit` — the micro tier.** One back-end function alone; every branch, every
boundary, empty/null, every error it throws. Never a component: the UI is proved by the composition tier.
Waits on the back-end ticket it tests. Skill: `framework-jest`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/tester-unit.md "Source — agents/tester-unit.md"
    }
    frontend = worker "frontend" {
      summary "Components, layout and styling, last in the line against functions already proved."
      description """
**Label `frontend-ui` → `frontend`.** Components, layout, styling, the shared component system. Last in the
line, built against functions already proved. Its landing is what unblocks `feature-test`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/frontend.md "Source — agents/frontend.md"
    }
    testerFeature = worker "tester-feature" {
      summary "Composition tier: one feature's pieces proved wired, its errors and successes surfacing."
      description """
**Label `feature-test` → `tester-feature` — the composition tier.** One feature's pieces proved wired; its
errors and successes surface. Never re-runs the micro tier's edge matrix, never walks cross-feature journeys.
Skill: `framework-playwright`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/tester-feature.md "Source — agents/tester-feature.md"
    }
    testerE2e = worker "tester-e2e" {
      summary "Broad tier: whole user journeys across features in a real browser on the real stack."
      description """
**Label `epic-e2e-test` → `tester-e2e` — the broad tier.** Journeys across features on the real stack:
cross-feature state handoffs, session continuity, one success and one failure journey per feature. The least
verbose tier. Skill: `framework-playwright`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/tester-e2e.md "Source — agents/tester-e2e.md"
    }
    reviewAgent = worker "review" {
      summary "Three questions from first principles, answered as comments on the pull request."
      description """
**Labels `feature-review` and `epic-review` → `review`.** Three questions from first principles: does the code
do what its source of truth demands, do the tests prove it, is it simple and readable. Maps the system the
change sits in (entry → exit, following imports), instruments it with trace logs, runs the tests against the
instrumented code, posts findings as PR comments. Files no tickets; the trace logs stay in the working tree.
Nothing is preloaded — `bead-read`, the standards and `pr-comments` load on demand at the step
that uses them.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/review.md "Source — agents/review.md"
    }
    leadAgent = worker "lead" {
      summary "The one worker no label reaches: stands on a gate, proves the tier, merges the branch through the forge."
      description """
**No label reaches this agent — a gate does.** `leadAgentName=\"lead\"` sits beside the `AGENT` map, not inside
it: the loop spawns the lead only from `dispatchOneGate`, for a ready gate whose tier flag is on, with the
identity `agent=lead label=gate:<tier>`. Everything below it has already been committed, pushed, reviewed and
closed; its whole job is to find out whether any of that is a lie.

**A closed ticket is a claim, not a fact.** Its proof is never the ticket list — it is four checks, each read
out with its numbers: every ticket that blocks the gate is closed; this tier's review actually reviewed and
every finding is answered on the branch; the project's own test command exits `0` **with a count above zero**
(the epic tier runs the end-to-end command too); the pull request's pipeline is green, where queued and skipped
are not green. Any one of them failing means it says *this gate is not proved*, comments the gate naming what is
missing, and touches no marker — the gate stays claimed and a human is told why.

**Merge, and only now** — `gh pr merge` / `glab mr merge` on that branch's PR into its **`prTarget`**, which
Step 1 derived off the gate's own id: the epic gate's `branch`, or `main` when the gate is the epic gate. The
forge does the merge: never a local `git merge` into the target, never a push to the target, never a rebase.
Where the target moved under the branch it brings the target *into this branch* (`git merge
origin/<prTarget>`),
commits the resolution with `bo commit` and pushes, so the PR carries it and the pipeline re-runs. Writes no
code and no tests; never closes, reopens, unclaims or reclaims a ticket — `bd close` would end the hook before
it ever ran.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/lead.md "Source — agents/lead.md"
    }
  }

  planAgents = layer "The plan agents (outside the tower)" {
    summary "The judgments the machine never makes: what the epic is, what a feature is, the order in one."
    description """
The judgments the machine never makes belong here: **what the epic is** (one `epic/<slug>` of two or three
lowercase words saying what the thing *is*); **what counts as a feature** (a complete user-facing flow, phased
foundations → core → dependent → polish); **the order inside a feature's line** (the logic, then its tests,
then the UI — never a fan); **the tier of a test ticket** (the label carries the tier, never `--agent`). Each
plan agent writes **one** metadata key and only one: `branch`, and only ever onto **its own gate**. The epic
planner names `epic/<slug>` on the epic gate, the feature planner names `feature/<slug>` on its own — and
nothing below a gate carries a copy of either, because a copy is a fact that can disagree with the gate that
decides it. The other half of what a plan agent writes is the **id**: every ticket below the epic is created
with an explicit dotted `--id`, because that id is the only thing the loop reads to find the two gates above
it. Nothing below recomputes a branch, and there is no parent chain to walk.

Each is one foreground command — `bo plan:epic`, `bo plan:feature`, `bo plan:task` — and anything you type
after it goes to the planner as the prompt. What runs them is on the agent-command node.
"""
    planEpic = planner "plan-epic" {
      summary "A vague idea into exactly one epic gate plus its end-to-end and review tickets."
      description """
Vague idea → clarifying questions → research → **exactly one** epic gate plus its `epic-e2e-test` and
`epic-review` tickets, and nothing else: **there is no merge ticket** and no label for one, because the lead
agent merges from the gate. It writes one key, `branch: epic/<slug>`, onto the gate alone; its two tickets
carry no metadata at all and are created with an explicit `--id`, `<epic>.<n>`, so their ids reach this gate.
The epic's own pull request aims at `main` — that is derived, not written, and it is the one merge in the tower
that reaches `main`. Then three edges: the suite blocks the gate, the review blocks the gate, the review waits
on the suite. Builds the tower and does no work in it.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/plan-epic.md "Source — agents/plan-epic.md"
    }
    planFeature = planner "plan-feature" {
      summary "An epic into complete user-facing flows, each with its own branch, suite and review."
      description """
Epic → feature-sized features (complete user-facing flows). Each gets its own `feature/<slug>`, written onto
its gate and nowhere else; its `feature-test` and `feature-review` tickets carry no metadata and are created
with an explicit `--id`, `<gate>.<m>`. Where a feature's pull request merges is never written here — the loop
truncates this gate's id at the first dot and reads the epic gate's `branch`; a feature with no epic gate above
it is not aimed at `main`, it is refused (`ERROR reason=no-epic-gate`). **No merge
ticket at this tier**: a feature has one branch and its tasks are the commits onto it. Four edges per feature —
the suite and the review each blocking the gate, the review waiting on the suite, and this feature's gate
blocking the epic's `epic-e2e-test`, which is what holds the epic tier off the frontier until every feature has
merged.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/plan-feature.md "Source — agents/plan-feature.md"
    }
    planTask = planner "plan-task" {
      summary "One feature into its tasks, chained one to the next, every one blocking the feature's gate."
      description """
One feature → its tasks, **labelled at creation** with `bd create -l <label>`, each one created with an
explicit `--id`, `<gate>.<m>`, and **no metadata at all** — not even a `branch`: the loop truncates that id
twice and reads both branches off the two gates it names (a plan agent never invents a branch below its own
tier, and refuses with `ERROR reason=metadata-missing` when the feature's gate carries no `branch` of its own).
Each task gets **two** edges —
onto the feature's gate, making it part of that tier, and onto the single task before it, ordering the build —
in one linked line, never a fan, and the tail hands to the `feature-test` ticket. Refuses outright without a
feature in the prompt: `ERROR reason=no-parent-feature`. Every ticket verified with a `bd show --json` round
trip, its `dependencies` among the fields checked.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/high_magic/blob/main/plugins/binary-obelisk/agents/plan-task.md "Source — agents/plan-task.md"
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE TOWER — a worked example. The machine above is verified against source;
  // this tower is an INVENTED example (a record box) used so the walkthroughs can
  // name real labels, branches and log lines.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  tower = layer "The tower — worked example: one epic, one feature" {
    summary "A worked example: one epic, one feature, nine tickets named by what they are."
    epic = gate "Epic gate" {
      summary "The epic gate — the what; closed once after the epic review, by a person or by the lead agent."
      description """
**What it is:** the epic gate — the *what*; closed once, after `epic-review`, by a person, or by the lead agent
when `BO_AGENT_EPIC_GATES=1`.

```
id:       box-7f3a                     ← no dot: this is the top of the tower
type:     gate                       (custom type: bd config set types.custom \"gate\")
labels:   none — a gate carries zero labels, so no label can ever dispatch anything to it
branch:   epic/record-box            ← plan-epic's key, and the only key this row carries
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/1 ← written by the LOOP, by ensureEpicGatePr
waits on: the epic review ticket      — an explicit blocked-by edge onto the gate itself
```

Two things about this row are **derived and never stored**. Its pull request aims at `main`, because the gate
its own id names is itself — `prTarget` is `main` exactly when the owner is the epic gate. And it is the
**epic** tier because nothing depends on it: `bd dep list box-7f3a --direction up -t blocks` comes back empty,
which is the only way the system knows a tier.

Every ticket of the tier blocks this gate — that set of blockers **is** the tier, and it is the list the lead
agent reads when it asks whether the epic is proved. What holds the *next* tier off the frontier is an explicit
edge onto the gate itself, and the gate stays open until someone verifies it — so the tower stalls here until a
person, or a lead agent with its tier flag on, closes it. `bd ready` never shows a gate: it is structurally
invisible, so there is nothing to exclude, and the loop claims it by status instead.
"""
      epicE2e = ticket "epic-e2e-test" {
        summary "Journeys across the whole app in a real browser, once every feature gate has closed."
        description """
```
title:    journeys across the whole app in a real browser
id:       box-7f3a.1               ← the next free number in the epic's one sequence; plan-epic ran first
label:    epic-e2e-test        → AGENT → tester-e2e
metadata: none                     ← the loop truncates this id and reads both branches off the epic gate
depends:  blocked-by every feature gate — one edge per feature, written by plan-feature
blocks:   the epic gate (its tier) and the epic review ticket
skill:    framework-playwright
```
**Brief:** state handoffs across the app, session continuity, one success and one failure journey through the
flow. Never re-test the micro tier's edges or the composition tier's wiring. Nothing is assembled for it: the
feature branches are already merged into `epic/record-box`, each by the lead agent standing on its own gate,
which is exactly why this ticket waits on the feature gates and not on a merge row.
"""
      }
      epicReview = ticket "epic-review" {
        summary "The three questions over the whole epic, posted on the epic pull request."
        description """
```
title:    the three questions over the whole epic
id:       box-7f3a.2
label:    epic-review          → AGENT → review
metadata: none                 ← same: its id reaches the epic gate, and the epic gate is its own target
depends:  blocked-by the end-to-end ticket
blocks:   the epic gate (its tier)
```
**Brief:** map entry → exit, instrument with trace logs, run the tests against the instrumented code, post
findings as PR comments. Then the epic gate is verified and closed — by a person, or by the lead agent proving
the tier and merging `epic/record-box` into `main` through the forge.
"""
      }
    }

    featA = gate "Feature gate" {
      summary "One complete flow — a user writes a record and sees it in the box; five tasks, one line."
      description """
```
id:       box-7f3a.3                   ← one dot: this row is its own feature, and it is below the epic
type:     gate
labels:   none — a gate carries zero labels
branch:   feature/add-record           ← plan-feature's key, and the only key this row carries
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/2  ← written by the Stop hook onto the ticket that first landed work
waits on: the feature review ticket
```

Again nothing here says where its pull request goes: the loop truncates `box-7f3a.3` at the first dot, reads
`epic/record-box` off that epic gate, and uses it as the **`prTarget`**. Nor does anything say it is a
**feature** gate — the epic's e2e ticket is blocked by it, so `bd dep list box-7f3a.3 --direction up -t blocks`
is non-empty, and that is the whole of the tier test.
One complete user-facing flow: a user writes a record and sees it in the box. Its five tasks are **one linked
line, never a fan**, so the frontier can only ever hand out one task per feature and this branch is never
written by two workers at once. Every one of them blocks this gate, and so do the suite and the review — that
set of blockers is the tier the lead agent proves before it merges `feature/add-record` into `epic/record-box`
through the forge, which is what closing this gate means.
"""
      backendA = ticket "backend-logic" {
        summary "validateRecord and saveRecord as pure functions: the first task, and the only one claimable."
        description """
```
title:    validateRecord + saveRecord — pure functions
label:    backend-logic        → AGENT → engineer
category: backend-logic
id:       box-7f3a.3.3             ← the next free number under this gate; its number is a place, not an order
metadata: none                     ← the loop reads both branches off the gates this id names
depends:  none — the first task, and the only ticket claimable when the run starts
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the unit-tests ticket
```
**Requirements**
1. `validateRecord(input): Either<RecordValidationError, Record>` — pure, no mutation
2. `saveRecord(validated)` through the repository port only
3. reject: empty title, title > 120 chars, duplicate slug, missing body

**Files:** `src/records/validate-record.ts`, `src/records/types.ts`, `src/records/ports.ts`
**Done means:** report in the final message; the Stop hook files it, not you.
"""
      }
      unitA = ticket "unit-tests" {
        summary "Exhaustive coverage of validateRecord: every branch, every boundary, every error."
        description """
```
title:    exhaustive coverage for validateRecord
label:    unit-tests           → AGENT → tester-unit
category: unit-tests
id:       box-7f3a.3.4
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the back-end ticket — the ticket it tests, its one and only predecessor
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the front-end ticket
skill:    framework-jest
```
**Cover:** every branch, every boundary (120/121 chars), empty and null input, each error it throws, the empty
body.
**Owned elsewhere:** composition wiring (feature tier), components (never here), journeys (epic tier).
"""
      }
      frontendA = ticket "frontend-ui" {
        summary "RecordForm and RecordCard against proved functions; its close unblocks the suite."
        description """
```
title:    RecordForm + RecordCard against proved functions
label:    frontend-ui          → AGENT → frontend
category: frontend-ux          ← the CATEGORY; the LABEL is frontend-ui
id:       box-7f3a.3.5
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the unit tests ticket
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the feature's suite — the tail of the chain hands to it
```
**Brief:** the form, validation feedback surfacing each `RecordValidationError`, the card in the box. Layout and
styling only — the logic is already proved by the micro tier. This ticket landing is what unblocks `feature-test`.
"""
      }
      featureTestA = ticket "feature-test" {
        summary "The add-a-record pieces proved wired, once every task has landed on the branch."
        description """
```
title:    the add-record pieces are wired
label:    feature-test         → AGENT → tester-feature
category: feature-test
id:       box-7f3a.3.1             ← a lower number than the tasks it waits on: plan-feature made this row first
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the front-end ticket — the LAST task; nothing is proved against half a feature
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the feature's review ticket
skill:    framework-playwright
```
**Cover:** the composed flow saves a record; each encapsulated error surfaces to the user.
"""
      }
      featureReviewA = ticket "feature-review" {
        summary "The three questions over feature/add-record; then the human verifies and closes the gate."
        description """
```
title:    the three questions over feature/add-record
label:    feature-review       → AGENT → review
category: review
id:       box-7f3a.3.2
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the feature's suite — no review before the suite
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier)
```
**Brief:** three questions from first principles, findings as PR comments. Then the feature gate is verified and
closed — by a person, or by the lead agent proving the tier and merging `feature/add-record` into
`epic/record-box` with `gh pr merge` / `glab mr merge` on that PR.
"""
      }
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE ALARMS — every reason string in the engine and both hooks, and what
  // happens to the ticket when it fires. All of them are walked in views.c4.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  alarms = layer "Alarms — every reason= in the code" {
    summary "Every reason the engine and both hooks can log, and what happens to the ticket."

    startupAlarms = layer "raised before the first tick — the run never starts" {
      summary "The alarms that refuse the run before its first tick."
      toolsMissing = alarm "startup: <tool> not found on PATH" {
        #fatal
        summary "A tool the loop needs is not on PATH, so the run exits before its first tick."
        description """
`requireToolsOnPath` wants `bd jq git` plus the first word of `BO_AGENT_COMMAND`, and `exit 1`s on the first
one missing. Note the Stop hook has its own list — `bd jq git bo` — shorter because it never spawns a session,
and carrying `bo` because that is the command it commits with.
"""
      }
      knobNotNumeric = alarm "startup: BO_<VAR>=<x> is not a number" {
        #fatal
        summary "A numeric knob was set to something that is not a number, so the run refuses to start."
        description """
`BO_MAX_PARALLEL`, `BO_POLL_S` or `BO_MAX_TICKETS` set to anything but digits. There is no defaulting silently:
a knob typed wrong would otherwise change how many workers run unattended.
"""
      }
      gateFlagNotASwitch = alarm "startup: BO_AGENT_<TIER>_GATES=<x> is not a switch" {
        #fatal
        summary "A gate tier switch is neither 0 nor 1, so the run refuses to guess who closes that tier's gate."
        description """
`BO_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` and `BO_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` are switches, not numbers: `0` means a person closes that
tier's gate, `1` means the lead agent does. Anything else — `true`, `yes`, `2` — prints the flag with both
meanings and exits 1. Checked after the numeric knobs, before the poll interval; unset is `0`, so this is always
a value somebody typed. There is no defaulting here either: a value read loosely would silently hand a tier's
merges to a machine, or silently withhold them from it.
"""
      }
      pollUnusable = alarm "startup: BO_POLL_S=<x> is unusable — it must divide 60" {
        #fatal
        summary "The poll interval does not divide 60, so the waiting-line cadence would be a lie."
        description """
The permitted list is printed: `1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60`. It must divide 60 because the waiting lines are
rate-limited at one per minute — `60 / BO_POLL_S` ticks — and a poll that does not divide the minute makes that
cadence a lie.
"""
      }
      noBeadsDb = alarm "startup: no beads database found from $PWD" {
        #fatal
        summary "This directory answers no ticket list, so nothing can be done here."
        description """
`bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an **array**. It is a real probe of \"can we do anything here\", not a
check for a `.beads` folder — `bd` walks **up** from the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`, so a database in the
environment does not save a wrong directory. `bo` runs the same probe before it even starts the engine.
"""
      }
      logDirUnwritable = alarm "startup: cannot create <dir>" {
        #fatal
        summary "The run's log directory cannot be created, so the run could never raise an alarm."
        description """
`mkdir -p $BO_LOG_ROOT/<timestamp>` failed. Fatal on purpose: a run that cannot write its logs cannot raise an
alarm, and a silent dispatcher is the one thing this design refuses to be.
"""
      }
      labelsStarved = alarm "not fatal: BO_ALLOWED_LABELS names nothing the map holds" {
        #fatal
        summary "The allowed labels name nothing the map holds: not fatal, but the tower never moves."
        description """
The one startup mistake that does **not** stop the run. A typo'd label narrows the allowed set to something no
ticket carries, so every claim matches zero keys, every claim is `label-ambiguous`, and the tower sits still
while the waiting lines keep arriving. Compare the files by hand: `bd list --json` labels against the map.
"""
      }
    }

    tickAlarms = layer "raised by the tick" {
      summary "Everything the tick can refuse, and whether the ticket stays claimed."
      bdFailure = alarm "ERROR reason=bd-failure exit=N" {
        summary "The claim failed or answered without an id; the tick returns and the next one tries again."
        description """
Two forms, one reason. `bd ready --claim` exited non-zero and the last line of `$logDir/bd-stderr` is quoted in
`msg=`; or it exited `0` and handed back no `id`, with the first 60 characters of the payload in `msg=`. The tick
returns; there is no claim to keep or return, so the next tick tries again. An **empty** answer is not this
alarm at all — that is a waiting line.
"""
      }
      labelAmbiguous = alarm "ERROR reason=label-ambiguous" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "Zero or two dispatching labels on the claim; the loop never guesses a worker."
        description """
Zero **or two** dispatching labels on a claim: `matched=\"…\" labels=\"…\"`. The dispatcher never guesses a
specialist. The ticket is LEFT CLAIMED so its 5-minute lease parks it off the frontier — otherwise the loop
would spin on the same unworkable ticket every tick — and the errors file is the alarm.

The commonest cause is a ticket labelled with its **category** instead of its label (`frontend-ux` for
`frontend-ui`), which matches nothing and reads identically to a ticket with no labels at all.
"""
      }
      noEpicGate = alarm "ERROR reason=no-epic-gate ticket=<id>" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The ticket's id reaches no gate: it belongs to no tower. The id is in the line."
        description """
Three forms, one reason, and the ticket's own id on every one of them — that is the point of the alarm, so a
person can find the row. The loop derives a ticket's whole git world by truncating its id, and truncation only
reaches a gate when the id is shaped like a tower's:

| form | when |
|---|---|
| (no `step=`) | the id carries **no dot** at all — a `bd create` with no `--id` mints exactly this |
| `gate=<id%%.*>` | truncating at the first dot gives a real ticket that is **not** a gate |
| `step=too-deep` | the id carries **three or more dots** — deeper than a tower has levels, so it names nothing |

Nothing was dispatched and the ticket is LEFT CLAIMED, so its five-minute lease parks it off the frontier
instead of the loop spinning on it. It is a plan bug, and it stays a plan bug where you can see it: the loop
never invents a gate and never walks a graph looking for one.
"""
      }
      ticketGateUnworkable = alarm "ERROR reason=gate-unworkable ticket=<id>" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The ticket's id reaches its gates, but one of them carries no branch."
        description """
Two forms, and the second carries `gate=`. `owner-carries-no-branch`: the gate this ticket's work lands on —
its feature gate, or the epic gate for an epic-tier ticket — has no `branch` of its own, so there is no folder
to work in and nothing to push. `epic-carries-no-branch`: the work has a home but the epic gate above it has no
`branch`, so the pull request has nothing to aim at.

Left claimed, same reason as the label alarm. The gate is the only place a branch is ever written, so a gate
with no `branch` is a plan bug, and the loop never guesses one to cover it.
"""
      }
      branchBusyAlarm = alarm "CLAIMS reason=branch-busy unclaimed" {
        #unclaimed
        summary "This ticket's branch is already at work: put the claim back, try the next one."
        description """
The branch this ticket wants is already checked out by a worker: `bd unclaim`, remember the ticket id in
`skippedTicket`, try the next claim — at most `BO_MAX_PARALLEL + 2` attempts a tick, then the waiting line
`branch_busy held=N`. One pair of hands per branch.

This is the **fallback**, not the mechanism: the graph already makes a feature one linked line, so the frontier
can only hand out one task per feature. The guard is for a tower that was never chained.
"""
      }
      epicPrFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=epic-pr-failed step=…" {
        #unclaimed
        summary "The epic branch or its pull request could not be published; seven steps say where."
        description """
The epic branch or its PR could not be published — and every feature PR below it aims at that branch, so nothing
beneath the epic has a pull-request target until this succeeds. Seven steps name exactly where it died:

| step | when |
|---|---|
| `gate-branch` | the epic gate carries no `branch` metadata of its own, so nothing beneath it has a pull-request target |
| `forge` | `detectForge` settled on nothing |
| `missing-tool` | the settled forge's CLI (`gh`/`glab`) is not installed |
| `seed` | the branch's own first commit could not be written — `git commit-tree` needs a `user.name` and a `user.email` |
| `push` | the epic branch could not be published off `main` |
| `pr` | no URL matching `/(pull\|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` came back |
| `annotate` | `bd update --set-metadata pr=` refused |

Nothing is walked to find the epic: the claim's id was already truncated into `$gateId` by
`resolveTicketBranches`, two lines earlier, and that is the gate this function is handed. So `gate-branch` is
the only plan-bug step here, and it is the same bug `gate-unworkable` raises one step earlier — a gate nobody
gave a branch.

A forge will not open a pull request whose head and base are the same commit, so the branch is born with one
commit of its own, `git commit-tree` off `main` — it never touches the tower's working tree and never moves its
`HEAD`. A branch already there is read first and left alone unless it sits on `main`'s own commit.

Every one of the seven releases the claim with `bd unclaim` and the next tick tries again — the ticket is
innocent whatever the step, because the failure is the machine's or the gate's and never the ticket's. No worker
is ever spawned into a branch that does not exist, because the claim is released before the spawn. The one place
the epic gate's own branch appears is `gateBranch=` on the line — never `branch=`, which belongs to the claim, and
the two differ.
"""
      }
      worktreeFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=worktree-failed step=…" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The branch's folder could not be built, so no worker is spawned into it; four steps say where."
        description """
`ensureWorktree` could not build the folder, so no worker is spawned into it. Left claimed, like a mislabelled
ticket: a worker spawned into a missing folder burns a slot on nothing. It self-heals what it can first — a stale
`git worktree` registration is pruned, and a folder left by an interrupted build is moved aside rather than
overwritten — and only then refuses.

| step | when |
|---|---|
| `no-start` | none of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` resolves to a commit |
| `stray-folder` | the branch's folder exists, is not a registered worktree, and could not be renamed aside |
| `gate` | the same failure on a **gate** dispatch — the line carries `gate=<id>`, and the gate is put back to `open` rather than left claimed, so the next tick may try it again |
| *(no step)* | `git worktree add` itself failed against the starting commit it did find |
"""
      }
      branchHasUnpushed = alarm "ERROR reason=branch-has-unpushed-commits" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The local branch holds commits no remote has seen; the loop refuses to reset a branch that does."
        description """
`git rev-list --count <branch> --not --remotes=origin` came back above zero: somebody's work sits on this local
branch and has never reached a remote. Rebuilding the folder means `git worktree add -B`, which **resets the
branch to the starting commit** — so the loop refuses instead of deleting a worker's commits. Left claimed.

The recovery is written into the line itself: `git reflog` to find them, or push them, then run the loop again.
This is the only worktree alarm that says what to do, because it is the only one where proceeding costs work
rather than time.
"""
      }
      folderMovedAside = alarm "ERROR reason=worktree-folder-moved-aside" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Not a refusal: an interrupted build's files were kept under a .stale-<epoch> name and the build went ahead."
        description """
The branch's folder existed, was not a registered worktree, and held files — the shape a build leaves when it
dies between creating the folder and registering it. `git worktree add` into a non-empty folder fails outright,
so the folder is renamed to `<path>.stale-<epoch>` and the branch is built fresh. The line is written to `errors`
and the worker **is** spawned: nothing was deleted, and the kept folder is named on the line.
"""
      }
      workerNotDispatched = alarm "ERROR reason=worker-not-dispatched" {
        #unclaimed
        summary "The spawn came back failed; the one alarm that puts the ticket straight back."
        description """
`$BO_AGENT_COMMAND --bg` came back non-zero; the last line of that worker's `spawn-<ticket>` file is quoted in `msg=`.
`bd unclaim` — unlike every other tick alarm, this one puts the ticket **straight back**, because nothing was
ever spawned to work it and the next tick may well succeed.

On a **gate** dispatch the line carries `gate=<id>` instead of a ticket, and the put-back is `moveGateTo <gate>
open in_progress` — the gate returns to `open`, off the ready list no longer, so the next tick may reach it
again.
"""
      }
      gateClaimLost = alarm "ERROR reason=gate-claim-lost gate=<id>" {
        #idle
        summary "The gate was no longer open the moment the loop reached for it; no lead agent was spawned."
        description """
The gate claim is `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open`, and `--if-status` is what makes it
safe: if the gate was no longer `open` the instant the loop reached for it — another loop got there first, or a
person closed it by hand — the write fails, the alarm fires, and **no lead agent is spawned**. Nothing is
spawned into a gate someone else already holds, and nothing is closed twice.

This is the only conditional write anywhere in the system, and it is also what takes a gate off the ready list
while its lead agent works: a gate never appears in `bd ready`, so status is the only handle on it. If that lead
agent dies, the failure hook parks the gate in `failed-agent` exactly as it parks a dead worker's ticket — and a
parked gate is no longer `open`, so it drops out of the ready-gate list and waits for a person.
"""
      }
    }

    stopAlarms = layer "raised by the Stop hook — fail-closed, in code order" {
      summary "Everything the Stop hook refuses, in code order: fail-closed, ticket untouched."
      missingToolHook = alarm "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=<t>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "bd, jq, git or bo is missing; checked before anything else the hook does."
        description """
`bd`, `jq`, `git` or `bo` missing — checked before anything else the hook does, but after the `BO_TICKET`
guard, so a session the loop never spawned still exits on line one. `bo` is there because the hook commits
through it, and the CLI is installed before any worker ever runs.
"""
      }
      noWorktreeHook = alarm "ERROR reason=no-worktree" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The hook was given no working directory, and it will not guess one."
        description """
The hook's stdin carried no `.cwd`, or that directory does not exist. The hook will not guess the worktree from
its own directory: guessing here means committing whatever is in some other project's tree.
"""
      }
      ticketNotInWork = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-in-work state=<s>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The ticket is not in progress; if it is closed, this is a second Stop and the hook leaves."
        description """
The ticket is not `in_progress`. Two readings: state `closed` means this is a second Stop in a session that
already shipped, and the hook leaves **silently** rather than committing onto a shared branch a second time;
anything else (`not-found`, `open`) is an alarm.
"""
      }
      noBranchHook = alarm "ERROR reason=no-branch" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The spawn carried no branch, so the hook refuses to file work anywhere."
        description """
`BO_BRANCH` was not on the spawn line — so the loop never got as far as deriving the branch off the ticket's id.
"""
      }
      noPrTarget = alarm "ERROR reason=no-pr-target" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The spawn carried no pull-request target, so the hook refuses to aim blind."
        description """
`BO_PR_TARGET` was not on the spawn. The loop derived that
target off the ticket's epic gate — the id truncated at the first dot, `main` when the owner is the epic gate
itself — and carried it through the claims line as `target=` and the spawn as `BO_PR_TARGET`; the hook reads the
claims line first and the spawn variable after it. An absent target means the hook would be aiming blind, and it
refuses to.
"""
      }
      notDeclaredDone = alarm "ERROR reason=not-declared-done" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The worker never touched its done marker: no commit, no push, no pull request, no close."
        description """
**The gate.** `$BO_DONE_FILE` does not exist: the session ended without saying, out loud, in the one place both
programs read, that its brief is finished. The session ending is not that statement — the marker is.

No commit, no push, no PR, no close. The ticket stays claimed so its lease parks it off the frontier and every
task chained behind it waits. That is the whole safety of a shared branch: a half-finished task cannot slide its
neighbours sideways. And the converse is the one failure this model cannot undo on its own — a worker that touches
the marker early files half a task onto a branch its siblings are queued behind.
"""
      }
      wrongBranch = alarm "ERROR reason=wrong-branch want=<b> got=<d>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The worker changed branch anyway, so the hook will not file its work under this ticket."
        description """
The worker was told never to change branches; if it did anyway, this is the last place that can refuse to file
its work under the wrong ticket's name. `git branch --show-current` must equal `BO_BRANCH` exactly — a detached
tree reads as `got=\"detached\"`.
"""
      }
      noTitle = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-has-no-title" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The commit message is the ticket title, and a titleless ticket cannot be filed."
        description """
The commit message IS the ticket title (`bd show --json --long | .title`), so a ticket with no title cannot be
filed — the commit would be anonymous on a shared branch.
"""
      }
      commitFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=commit-failed" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Committing the tree failed — a genuinely empty tree is allowed, this is not one."
        description """
`git add -A` then `git commit -F -`, message = the ticket title plus a `Ticket: <id>` trailer. A review or test
ticket may legitimately land nothing (`git diff --cached --quiet` skips the commit entirely and the hook carries
on to push, PR and close), so this is a genuine commit failure, not an empty tree.
"""
      }
      pushFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=push-failed" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Pushing the branch to the remote failed; its output is quoted on the line."
        description """
`git push -u origin <BO_BRANCH>` failed; the first 160 characters of its output are the `msg=`.
"""
      }
      forgeUnknown = alarm "ERROR reason=forge-unknown remote=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Nothing settled which forge this remote is, and the hook refuses to guess."
        description """
All three settle steps came up empty — the remote name lied, it publishes neither `refs/pull/` nor
`refs/merge-requests/`, and the machine holds both `gh` and `glab`, or neither. The hook refuses to guess a forge.
Reached **after** the commit and the push, because the hook only needs the forge to open a PR.
"""
      }
      forgeToolMissing = alarm "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=gh|glab forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The forge is known, but its command line tool is not installed."
        description """
The forge settled, its CLI is not installed. Same reason as the up-front check, different `tool=` and with
`forge=` appended — which is how you tell the two apart in the errors file.
"""
      }
      prNotFoundGate = alarm "ERROR reason=pr-not-found forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The lead agent stopped on a gate whose branch has no pull request at all — the gate path's own refusal."
        description """
Reached only when the claim's `kind` is `gate`. The gate path never opens a pull request — the lead agent was sent
to **merge** one, so the hook only looks the branch's PR up at any state, `--state all` on GitHub and `--all` on
GitLab, against that branch and its `prTarget`. Nothing matches: there was nothing for the lead agent to merge,
and it declared done anyway. The gate stays claimed and the tier behind it waits.

This is the reason that does not exist on the task path, and the two are not the same failure: `pr-not-created`
means the hook could not raise a pull request, this means one was never there to merge. Only two things ever open
one — the loop, for the **epic** branch, the first time it claims anything under that epic (`epic-published`),
and the Stop hook, for a **feature** branch, the first time work lands on it. So a feature gate reaching this
line means no worker's work ever landed on that branch and its tier was closed some other way, by a hand and not
by the hook.
"""
      }
      prNotCreated = alarm "ERROR reason=pr-not-created forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Nothing came back that looks like a pull request url."
        description """
Nothing came back that looks like a PR url. Either the list of the branch's existing PRs and the create both
failed, or the CLI printed an error quoting some other URL — only `/(pull|merge_requests)/<digits>` counts.
"""
      }
      ticketNotAnnotated = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated pr=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Writing the pull request url onto the ticket failed, so the hook will not close it."
        description """
`bd update --set-metadata pr=<url>` failed. The work is pushed but the ticket cannot say where it went, so the
hook stops rather than closing a ticket whose PR is unfindable. (`--set-metadata` **merges**, so this never
disturbs the plan's `branch` key.)
"""
      }
      closeFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=close-failed pr=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The final close failed: the work is out there with no record in the tower."
        description """
`bd close <ticket> --session <session_id> --reason \"worker session … finished; PR …\"` failed. The last step, and
the only one where everything else already succeeded — the commit, the push and the PR are all out there with no
record in the tower. `bd` refuses to close a ticket whose assignee is not the actor, and the dispatcher claimed
this one, so the hook deliberately passes the real `session_id` rather than overriding the actor.
"""
      }
    }

    failAlarms = layer "raised by the StopFailure hook" {
      summary "Where the StopFailure hook can fail while parking a dead worker's ticket."
      ticketNotFoundFail = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-found" {
        summary "The ticket the spawn named is gone or not in progress; the hook leaves it alone."
        description """
`bd show --json` for `BO_TICKET` returned no state at all. Anything not `in_progress` (already parked, already
shipped) is somebody else's business and the hook leaves silently — a dead session must not rewrite a ticket
another worker owns.
"""
      }
      failNotAnnotated = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated kind=<k>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The reason could not be written, so the hook stops before touching the status."
        description """
The `--append-notes` writing the reason failed, so the hook stops before touching the status: a ticket parked in
failure with nothing said about why is worse than one left `in_progress`.
"""
      }
      statusNotSet = alarm "ERROR reason=status-not-set kind=<k> from=<state>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The reason is on the ticket but its status never changed: looks live, is dead."
        description """
`bd update --status failed-agent` refused, so the reason is on the ticket but its status is still `in_progress` —
the shape that looks like a live worker and is a dead one. Nothing here will bring it back: no `bd reclaim` runs
in this plugin, so it sits out of `bd ready` until a person moves it, and the missing `failures` line is the tell.

**This alarm almost always means one thing: the database never registered the status.** `bd` accepts an unknown
type and an unknown status silently and then refuses to set it, so `failed-agent` needs
`bd config set status.custom "failed-agent:frozen"` — which `bead-initialize` now runs beside the `gate` type.
Both settings are **per database**, so a tower created before that line existed raises this alarm on every dead
worker: run the two `bd config set` lines in it. Until then the ticket never parks, and the reaper cannot help —
`bd reclaim` only reverts issues that are `in_progress`.
"""
      }
    }
  }

  waiting = layer "Waiting lines — nothing failed, nothing ran (one a minute per reason)" {
    summary "The quiet ticks: nothing failed, nothing ran, one line a minute per reason."
    frontierEmpty = waitingLine "reason=frontier_empty" {
      #idle
      summary "Nothing waits anywhere: the tower is finished or unplanned. The calm one."
      description """
The claim came back empty and the stall probe found **no** off-frontier work: the tower is finished, or nobody has
planned anything yet. This is the calm one.
"""
    }
    towerStalled = waitingLine "reason=tower_stalled waiting=N" {
      #idle
      summary "Work exists but a blocker holds it off the frontier — usually a gate whose tier is still a person's."
      description """
The other half of an empty frontier, and the two need different alarms: work tickets **do** exist
(`bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0` counted N of them) but an active blocker holds them off `bd ready` —
an open gate two levels below, most often. That is the expected shape of a run: a gate is a checkpoint, and while
its tier's flag is `0` nothing dispatches it, so everything behind it waits for a person. `bd list` hides closed
tickets and gates by default, so N counts work tickets only. A broken tower must never read as a calm one.
"""
    }
    branchBusyWaiting = waitingLine "reason=branch_busy held=N" {
      #idle
      summary "Every claimable ticket wanted a branch already at work, so all were put back."
      description """
Tickets were claimable and every one of them wanted a branch already at work: N were unclaimed and skipped this
tick. The graph makes a feature sequential, so this is the fallback for a tower that was never chained.

The same reason arrives from the gate dispatch too, and there it reads `held=1 gate=<id>`: the gate's own branch is
already at work, so the gate is left `open` and untouched — the busy check runs **before** the claim, so unlike
every other gate failure this one never moves the gate's status at all.
"""
    }
    gateUnworkable = waitingLine "reason=gate_unworkable gate=<id> step=<why>" {
      #idle
      summary "A ready gate's id reaches no usable branch and target, so no lead agent can be placed or aimed."
      description """
A gate reached the frontier and `resolveTicketBranches` refused its id. `step=owner-carries-no-branch` — the gate
carries no `branch` of its own, so nowhere to put the tier's work; `epic-carries-no-branch` — the epic gate above
it carries none, so its pull request has nothing to aim at; `not-a-gate` — truncating the id at the first dot
lands on something that is not a gate; `too-deep` — the id carries three or more dots. Same rule as the ticket
path, same refusal: the loop never invents a branch and never guesses a target. The consequence is quieter here —
with no lead agent spawned the gate is never claimed, so it stays `open`, shows up ready again next tick, and the
tier behind it simply waits. An alarm would fire every tick forever; this is a line, rate-limited to one a minute.
"""
    }
    maxParallel = waitingLine "reason=max_parallel in_progress=N" {
      #idle
      summary "The cap is reached and tickets are in progress, so this tick claims nothing."
      description """
`BO_MAX_PARALLEL` background sessions are already alive and N tickets are in progress — the workers are working,
so the tick simply does not claim.
"""
    }
    maxParallelStalled = waitingLine "reason=max_parallel_stalled in_progress=0" {
      #idle
      summary "At capacity with nothing in progress: the counted workers are ghosts."
      description """
At capacity with **nothing in progress**: the slots are ghosts — sessions counted busy whose tickets are gone. The
count comes fresh from `$BO_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` every tick precisely so this state is visible rather than silent.
"""
    }
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // The depends-on edges, as edges of the model rather than of one walkthrough.
  // A static view draws only these; a dynamic view draws only the edges its own
  // steps create — so the graph view needs them here. The edges that make a TIER
  // are absent, every one of them: each row blocks its own gate, and a gate is
  // drawn as the box around its tier's rows, so the engine refuses those edges as
  // parent-child. The gate's own panel is the list of its blockers.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  tower.featA.backendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.unitA "then its tests"
  tower.featA.unitA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.frontendA "then the interface"
  tower.featA.frontendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureTestA "then the suite"
  tower.featA.featureTestA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureReviewA "then the review"

  tower.featA -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicE2e "no journey over an unmerged flow"
  tower.epic.epicE2e -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicReview "the journeys, then the review"
}
